# CAPTURE OWNERSHIP — the SIBLING-EVALUATION-ORDER cell. A closure captures a
# collection in one argument of an aggregate init while a SIBLING argument of
# the same init reallocates that collection.
#
# The capture and the realloc happen inside one `Pair(…)` construction, so this
# cell adds an emission-ORDER dimension (D10(b): reason about when a thing
# happens relative to its siblings, which is only visible in the IR) on top of
# the capture-boundary rule. It does not change the root: the same program with
# no aggregate at all behaves identically, and both are answered by the
# environment holding its own value.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THE WRONG ANSWER LOOKS LIKE HERE. Without the rule this is not a crash
# — it is rc 0 printing `7` then `70`, a silently wrong number over a
# use-after-free, where both the language definition and `ggdef run` say `7`
# then `7`. A crash becoming a wrong number is not an improvement (Core #8),
# which is why the second line is asserted rather than the exit code.
#
# ⚠ THE REALLOC IS LOAD-BEARING — 5000 pushes, not one.
#
# ⚠ AND THE INSTRUMENT IS C + ASan: under `--backend=llvm --sanitize` this shape
# reports only a leak, no use-after-free (`todo/t0731`, the sweep's second-lane
# gap). `p.f.clone()` is deliberately live — `gorget_closure_clone_to_owned`
# shallow-copies the 16-byte `{fn_ptr, env}` handle, so the clone and the
# original share one environment and the cell also covers that aliasing.
#
# Prints `7` then `7`, both backends.
struct Pair:
    Callable[int(int)] f
    int n

int mutate(Vector[int] &v):
    int i = 0
    while i < 5000:
        v.push(i)
        i = i + 1
    return 0

void main():
    Vector[int] v = [1]
    Pair p = Pair((int x): x + v.get(0).unwrap(), mutate(&v))
    Callable[int(int)] g = p.f.clone()
    print(6 + v.get(0).unwrap())
    print(g(6))
